    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e$3.17 a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost). The dried chiles carry a real cost the first time, but they are shelf stable for a year, so every batch after this one is cheaper.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eDried chiles from the Hispanic aisle, in the bag.\u003c/strong\u003e Ancho and guajillo in the big bags cost a fraction of the branded spice-rack packets, and they keep a year sealed in the pantry.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003ePork shoulder is the right cut and the cheap cut.\u003c/strong\u003e A long braise in acidic chile sauce is exactly what a tough shoulder wants. A lean loin would dry out here.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eMarinate overnight. It is free.\u003c/strong\u003e The vinegar in the puree tenderizes the pork while you sleep, which means less cooking time and better texture for zero extra dollars.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eVinegar and a spoon of sugar do the balancing.\u003c/strong\u003e Cheap pantry items keep the chile from turning bitter, which is the mistake that ruins most homemade red chile."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Stem and seed the dried ancho and guajillo chiles, toast them briefly in a dry pan, then soak in hot water for 20 minutes until fully soft.",
                    "Blend the chiles with some soaking water, the garlic, white vinegar, soy sauce, sugar, ground cumin, dried oregano, dried thyme, salt and black pepper into a thick smooth puree, then strain out the skins.",
                    "Cut the pork shoulder into large cubes, coat every piece in the red puree, and refrigerate overnight.",
                    "Brown the diced onion in the vegetable oil in a heavy oven-safe pot, then add the pork with all of its marinade and the bay leaves.",
                    "Cover and braise at 325F for about 3 hours, stirring once an hour, until the pork shreds under a fork and the sauce has gone dark and thick.",
                    "Cook the rice per the package, then taste the chile for salt and pull out the bay leaves before you portion."
                ],
